the former co-owner of a Massachusetts  compounding pharmacy has been sentenced  to nine years in federal prison for his  role in a deadly meningitis outbreak  Barry Cadden was head pharmacist of the  New England compounding center at the  time of the 2012 outbreak 64 people died  nationwide and hundreds of others became  sick Jim Axelrod has been following this  story from the very beginning Jim good  morning  oh good morning in March cotton was  convicted on conspiracy and fraud  charges but not on acts of second degree  murder related to racketeering however  the way the judge handled the verdict  may have allowed Cadden to escape a much  harsher sentence something that has  angered the families who lost loved ones  in this case Sharron windgates husband  died after receiving a contaminated  steroid shot Darryl Nealon lost his  father me my brother my sister he was  our foundation  Robyn Hannah bath survived but says she  still suffers from the effects of the  tainted shots I can't go Barry Cadden  left court after the sentencing without  saying a word you think it was a fair  sentence Barry but inside he had made a  tearful apology to those affected saying  I am so sorry for your extraordinary  losses a dozen victims gave emotional  impact statements I think nobody could  hear those statements without being  moved to tears many of us were the US  Attorney's Office was also hoping for a  longer punishment urging the judge to  sentence Cadden to 35 years this was an  unusual verdict slip the final verdict  form revealed the jury was split on  multiple charges including the more  serious acts of second-degree murder  related to racketeering typically that  would prompt the judge to send them back  for more deliberations the judge did not  question the numbers on the form the  jury affirmed the verdict and the case  was closed I believe that justice has  failed  don't I really do the victims families  tell us they feel like the judge robbed  them of not only a longer sentence for  Cadden potentially life in prison but  they also feel robbed of the justice and  hearing the word guilty in relation to  the deaths of their loved ones well it's  tough for those families you can clearly  see and hear their pain doing that story  thank you Jim
 

